1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,653
A robot setting a table looks impressive. But move a cup after the plan

2
00:00:03,653 --> 00:00:07,307
starts, and the real test begins. Can it notice the change, stop an

3
00:00:07,307 --> 00:00:10,960
unsafe action, and still finish the job? This is Granted Robotics, created

4
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:14,614
by Shivam Gupta.

5
00:00:15,314 --> 00:00:19,274
For robotics teams, the difficult work often starts after a demonstration

6
00:00:19,274 --> 00:00:23,234
fails. What changed? Which action caused the problem? Did the recovery

7
00:00:23,234 --> 00:00:27,195
actually complete the task? Granted makes that investigation visible

8
00:00:27,195 --> 00:00:31,155
through a familiar two arm table setting scenario.

9
00:00:31,855 --> 00:00:35,985
The workbench puts the mission, workspace, and execution evidence together.

10
00:00:35,985 --> 00:00:40,116
A reviewer can explore the task, inspect a disturbance, and understand

11
00:00:40,116 --> 00:00:44,246
the recovery sequence. The goal is to make the important decisions readable,

12
00:00:44,246 --> 00:00:48,377
instead of asking a judge to trust a success animation.

13
00:00:49,077 --> 00:00:53,715
Under the interface, a Python backend uses MuJoCo for articulated arms,

14
00:00:53,715 --> 00:00:58,353
tabletop objects, and simulated grasp constraints. An OpenVINO graph

15
00:00:58,353 --> 00:01:02,990
evaluates geometric safety signals. The browser is the presentation

16
00:01:02,990 --> 00:01:07,628
layer. Simulation evidence is separate from that visual explanation.

17
00:01:08,328 --> 00:01:12,853
Across ten randomized seeds, grip loss completed ten out of ten times

18
00:01:12,853 --> 00:01:17,378
with recovery, and zero out of ten without it. We varied object placement,

19
00:01:17,378 --> 00:01:21,903
mass, and friction. These are fifty local simulation runs, with deterministic

20
00:01:21,903 --> 00:01:26,427
control. We have not yet demonstrated on Intel Core Ultra hardware.

21
00:01:27,127 --> 00:01:30,645
The first customer hypothesis is robotics integrators. A paid pilot

22
00:01:30,645 --> 00:01:34,162
would import one task and reproduce its recurring failures. We would

23
00:01:34,162 --> 00:01:37,679
measure engineering time spent diagnosing regressions. A five hundred

24
00:01:37,679 --> 00:01:41,196
dollar monthly workspace is a pricing experiment, not a claim of customer

25
00:01:41,196 --> 00:01:44,714
demand or revenue.

26
00:01:45,414 --> 00:01:49,541
Granted Robotics makes the recovery part of the product. It gives teams

27
00:01:49,541 --> 00:01:53,669
a clear place to inspect what failed, how the system responded, and

28
00:01:53,669 --> 00:01:57,797
what the evidence supports. Explore the workbench and the open source

29
00:01:57,797 --> 00:02:01,925
implementation. Created by Shivam Gupta for the AI Infra Summit Hackathon.
